<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · region</p><h1 class="enc-title">Blackfeet Reservation</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Blackfeet Indian Reservation</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Blackfeet Reservation is the home of the Blackfeet Nation, the Piikani or Amskapi Pikuni, in north-western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/montana">Montana</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a>, east of Glacier National Park, with its headquarters at Browning. Its lands are the remnant of the large territory recognised to the Blackfoot by the Lame Bull Treaty of 1855, reduced by later agreements in the 1870s, 1880s and 1890s.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/blackfeet-reservation.jpg" alt="Blackfeet Reservation" width="320" height="213" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Murray Foubister · CC BY-SA 2.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Blackfeet_Indian_sign_(15987533967).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>The reservation lies east of Glacier National Park and borders <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alberta">Alberta</a>, with Cut Bank Creek and Birch Creek forming part of its eastern and southern edges. It covers 3,000 square miles, twice the size of the national park, in parts of Glacier and Pondera counties. Elevations run from 3,400 feet to 9,066 feet at Chief Mountain. The east is mostly open grassland, while a narrow strip along the western edge is forested with fir and spruce, and the St. Mary, Two Medicine and Milk rivers drain the land.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The Blackfeet have lived on the Northern Great Plains for thousands of years. Having acquired horses and firearms through raids and trade with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cree">Cree</a>, they controlled lands from north of present-day Edmonton to Yellowstone and from Glacier to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/black-hills">Black Hills</a>. In the late 19th century European Americans and Canadians encroached on this territory and forced them to cede it. A treaty of 1896 left the Badger-Two Medicine area, sacred to the Blackfeet as their place of creation and origin, outside their lands, though they kept rights to hunt, fish and gather there.</p><p>From the early 1980s the tribe worked to undo drilling leases the Bureau of Land Management had sold in the Badger-Two Medicine without consulting it. A moratorium on new permits was made permanent in 2007, and in November 2016 the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/departement">Department</a> of the Interior cancelled the 15 leases held by Devon Energy. The last leaseholder, Solenex, settled in 2023 for $2.3 million, ending its activities on the land. In September 2014 the Blackfeet Nation was one of the original eight signatories of the Buffalo Treaty, signed on the reservation.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>The 2010 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/census">census</a> counted 10,405 people living on the reservation, and the Blackfeet Nation has 16,500 enrolled members. Browning is the seat of tribal government, which is run by the elected Blackfeet Tribal Business Council. Oil and gas leases are the main source of income, alongside tourism, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cattle">cattle</a> ranching and seasonal wildfire fighting. The annual festival of North American Indian Days is held near the Museum of the Plains Indian in Browning.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Blackfeet Nation” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Today they live on the Siksika, Kainai and Piikani reserves in Alberta and the <b>Blackfeet Reservation</b> in Montana.</blockquote><a href="/state/blackfoot-confederacy">Blackfoot Confederacy</a></li></ul></section>
